@q 17:12-16

Urbanization **lowers both birth and death rates** (better health care, later marriage, more literacy). It has **not been uniform** across regions; big cities have grown fastest.

@section 25.2 | Phases and Definitions

@q 17:17-19

@src1 17:3
@one U.P.P.C.S. (Pre) 2009
Which of the following conditions determine an area as urban as given in the Census Report of 2001? 1. Its minimum population will have to be 5,000. 2. The minimum percentage of its working male population, engaged in non-agricultural work, will have to be 75. 3. The density of its population will have to be at least 400 persons per sq. km. 4. It will have to have a minimum area of 10 sq. km.
@ans 1, 2 and 3
@end

@table
Urban definition (Census) | Detail
**Statutory town** | Any place with a **municipality, corporation, cantonment board** or notified town area
**Census town** | Population **5,000+**, **75%+ of male workers** in non-farm work, density **400+ per sq km**
Census 2011 | **4,041 statutory towns** and **3,894 census towns** (7,935 towns in all)
Phases | 1901-31 slow; **1931-61 moderate**; 1961 onwards rapid
Fastest urban growth decade | **1971-81** (46%)
@end

